{"archetypes":[{"name":"redirect-transform","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Three launchers with different variable names each need a stdout/stderr split redirect; an off-by-one or wrong variable name silently breaks auth/transient failure detection or discards events entirely.","prompt_body":"Examine the proposed redirect transformation in each of the three launchers separately: `launch-review.sh`, `launch-codex-implement.sh`, and `launch-codex-ci.sh`. For each, verify the exact before/after redirect syntax is syntactically valid Bash 3.2 (e.g., `>> \"$SIDECAR\" 2>&1` vs `> \"${OUTPUT}.events.jsonl\" 2> \"$SIDECAR\"`) and that the variable names used for the events sidecar path (`${OUTPUT}.events.jsonl`, `${TRANSCRIPT_PATH}.events.jsonl`) match the actual variable names in the current launcher code rather than assumed names. Check that `external_is_auth_failure` and `external_is_transient_infra_failure` calls still receive stderr text in SIDECAR after stdout is split away. Verify the `/dev/null` branch in `launch-review.sh` handles the events sidecar consistently (events discarded, parse-codex-usage returns 1, no ledger row) and that this behavior is correctly reflected in the test stubs. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stub-audit","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan names seven specific line numbers in test-launch-review.sh for codex stubs to update; a missed or misidentified stub leaves old `tokens used` behavior exercising the deleted code path in tests that still pass.","prompt_body":"Cross-check the seven line numbers cited in the plan for codex stubs in `scripts/test-launch-review.sh` (174, 559, 603, 618, 803, 872, 973) against the actual file content to verify no stubs are at different lines, missing from the list, or duplicated. For each stub location, confirm that the plan's proposed JSONL replacement event shape (`{\"msg\":{\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":N,...}}}`) also satisfies the `--output-last-message` file write so existing transcript-shape assertions still pass. Verify that `scripts/test-launch-codex-ci.sh` stub updates include at least one fail-closed branch (stub emits no JSONL) asserting the token-record is empty, and one success branch asserting per-bucket fields. Check that the Makefile target addition wires `test-parse-codex-usage` into the aggregating target that already includes `test-launch-review` and `test-token-vendor-scrapers`. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"fail-closed-chain","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Replacing the aggregate fallback with fail-closed semantics threads through token-ledger, token-report, render-cost-line, and token-cost; a gap in the chain means BLENDED_WARN could still fire or cost lines silently disappear for operators on older Codex CLI versions.","prompt_body":"Trace the fail-closed contract end-to-end: when `parse-codex-usage.sh` returns 1, the plan asserts that no ledger row is written and therefore `BUCKETS_codex.total == 0`, which prevents `BLENDED_WARN` from firing at `token-cost.sh:217-222`. Verify this chain against the actual `token-cost.sh` condition logic: confirm the guard is `vendor_aggregate > 0 AND per-bucket sum == 0`, not just `per-bucket sum == 0`, so that a zero aggregate row does not trigger the warning. Check whether the `launch-codex-ci.sh` path that pre-touches `${OUTPUT}.token-record` (line 177 in the plan's description) can leave an empty file that `append-token-record.sh` still processes, potentially writing a zero-total Codex row that re-triggers the aggregate-only BLENDED_WARN path. Confirm that the `test-token-vendor-scrapers.sh` extension asserts BLENDED_WARN still fires on a legacy `record-vendor codex total=N` (no per-bucket fields) row, which proves the aggregate-fallback detection regression case is covered and not silently skipped. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
